// GitHub issue #462: the second lock class, and the first lock in this
// kernel that does not mask interrupts.
//
// kernel/lib/mutex.tkb masks DAIF.I on every acquire, which is what makes
// a Mutex safe to take from an interrupt handler -- a handler can never
// interrupt a holder on the same core. It is also what makes it wrong for
// a lock held across real work: masking is all-or-nothing, so the whole
// kernel's interrupt latency becomes the longest critical section anyone
// writes.
//
// A TcpConnection is held across a user copy AND a frame transmit
// (kernel_tcp_send_received takes the TX permission). Masking there is the
// same defect issue #452 had to restructure the retransmit path to avoid,
// and here it cannot be restructured away, because the transmit is what
// the syscall is for.
//
// So this class trades the property it does not need for the one it does:
// **no interrupt handler may take a TaskMutex**, and in exchange holding
// one costs no interrupt latency at all. GitHub issue #449 decided that
// whether a handler may take a lock is a property OF THE LOCK, declared
// where the lock is; a separate type is that declaration, and reaching for
// the wrong acquire is not expressible rather than merely wrong.
//
// Current limitations (2026-11-10). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - THE INTERRUPT RULE IS CHECKED for the spinning acquire entries:
//   ordinary task_mutex_acquire carries may_block; short and boot carry
//   no_handler_lock. Neither effect permits an interrupt-handler caller.
//   All three currently spin without masking interrupts. The short entry's
//   caller must retain its preempt-count discipline; the boot entry borrows
//   the real BootPhase and is confined to the snapshot guard factory by the
//   compiled caller map. That void leaf alone retains no phase loan after
//   returning: the factory's scoped returned linear guard retains the loan.
//   task_mutex_trylock does not spin and has neither handler-ban effect.
//   A handler that loops on trylock is still not distinguished from one
//   that gives up.
// - NO OWNER, NO RECURSION COUNT, NO DEADLOCK DETECTION -- same as Mutex.
//   Taking the same one twice on one core hangs.
// - NO LEVEL, and unlike Mutex this one will never need one. A level
//   exists to mask fewer interrupts; this masks none.
// - THE RANK IS FIXED HERE at 30 (connection), so every adopter's
//   minimum reachable rank is 30 whatever its own wrapper declares. All
//   adopters rank below the IRQ level (rank 40), the floor of the
//   masking locks, which is the band rule kernel/CONCURRENCY.md states (2026-10-25, #966: the ext2
//   mutation lock, the block device, TCP connections and GuardedField;
//   #1071: the TCP frames and retransmit locks, the GEM, virtio and xHCI
//   ring slots, and InitOnce). A wrapper over this that declares a higher
//   rank still reaches 30, so its guard may not be held while another
//   TaskMutex is taken: those wrappers declare 30 too.
// - THE RAW RELEASE IS THE CALLER'S OBLIGATION: these void leaves do not
//   mint a guard. Wrappers such as BlockDeviceGuard, Ext2MutationGuard,
//   InitOnceGuard, GuardedFieldGuard and TcpConnectionOwner carry their own
//   linear ownership and reviewed acquisition/release pairing. The snapshot
//   boot factory also returns the existing KernelLogSnapshotGuard tied to
//   its borrowed phase. A linear wrapper's consumption does not itself
//   prove that its implementation unlocks the correct physical word.
use "kernel/lib/spinlock.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/boot_once.tkb";

// One field, at offset 0, for the same reason Mutex has one: a lock address
// names its AtomicWord lock word.
struct no_copy TaskMutex { private word: AtomicWord; }

// Free. Call once, before it is reachable by anything else.
fn task_mutex_init(m: *TaskMutex @ id) {
    static_assert(offsetof(TaskMutex, word) == 0,
                  "TaskMutex.word must be first: a lock address names its word");
    atomic_word_store(&m.word, 0);
}

// Takes it. No mask, so nothing to hand back -- which is why the caller's
// token needs no flags field and why this pair is not shaped like Mutex's.
//
// `may_block` is not decoration and not an approximation. From an
// interrupt handler this genuinely blocks forever: the handler spins for a
// holder that cannot run until the handler returns. The effect system
// already rejects `may_block` on an `!{interrupt}` function and reports
// the call path, so the rule this file's header states is now checked
// rather than asserted -- which is what GitHub issue #449 owed it.
//
// linux_user/intrusive_pool/intrusive_pool_lib.tkb had already worked out that this is what
// the annotation would do, and declined it there because a pool lock IS
// safe from a handler. This is the case it declined it FOR.
fn task_mutex_acquire(m: *TaskMutex @ id)
        !{locks, may_block, unsafe, acquires_lock_30_connection} {
    spin_lock(&m.word);
}

// Explicit bootstrap entry: the caller carries the existing boot authority
// to this acquisition. It never parks and never inspects scheduler context.
// The enclosing typed guard factory must tie its returned guard to phase.
fn task_mutex_acquire_boot(m: *TaskMutex @ id, phase: borrow BootPhase[boot])
        !{locks, no_handler_lock, unsafe, acquires_lock_30_connection} {
    spin_lock(&m.word);
}

// GitHub issue #1350: the same acquire for a holder that holds the preempt
// count and reaches no wait, so a waiter never waits for this core. A handler
// still must not take it.
fn task_mutex_acquire_short(m: *TaskMutex @ id)
        !{locks, no_handler_lock, unsafe, acquires_lock_30_connection} {
    spin_lock(&m.word);
}

fn task_mutex_release(m: *TaskMutex @ id) !{locks, unsafe} {
    spin_unlock(&m.word);
}

// One attempt. For a caller with something else to do rather than wait,
// and the only way to observe the exclusion with one thread.
fn task_mutex_trylock(m: *TaskMutex @ id) -> bool
        !{locks, unsafe, acquires_lock_30_connection} {
    return spin_trylock(&m.word);
}

fn task_mutex_is_held(m: *TaskMutex @ id) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return spin_lock_is_held(&m.word);
}
